.TH PALADIN-TUI 1 "2026-07-24" "paladin 0.1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
paladin-tui \- interactive terminal front-end for paladin
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B paladin-tui
.RI [ FILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B paladin-tui
is a full-screen, keyboard-driven terminal interface over the same
.BR paladin (1)
core. It presents one form with four mode tabs \(em Encrypt, Decrypt, Info, and
Verify \(em and runs each operation off the UI thread, streaming a progress
gauge while it works. The file format, cryptography, and defaults are identical
to the command-line tool: the default cipher is AES-256-GCM and the default
key-derivation function is Argon2id.
.PP
An optional
.I FILE
argument prefills the input-path field; otherwise the form starts empty.
.PP
A failed authentication tag is reported as a single condition \(em
.I "wrong password or corrupted/tampered file"
\(em because the two are cryptographically indistinguishable.
.SH MODES
The mode tabs are selected with
.B \(<-
/
.B \(->
while the tabs are focused. Each mode shows only the fields it needs.
.TP
.B Encrypt
Encrypt the input file. Fields: input, output, password, and a confirm field
that must match.
.TP
.B Decrypt
Decrypt the input file. Fields: input, output, password.
.TP
.B Info
Print unauthenticated header metadata (cipher, KDF and parameters, version,
flags, chunk size, and a stored filename when present and well-formed) into a
results pane. No password is read; the only field is the input path.
.TP
.B Verify
Decrypt and discard the output to verify integrity and the secret. Nothing is
written. Fields: input, password.
.PP
The output path is prefilled automatically \(em on encrypt from the input name
plus
.I .paladin
(or
.I .paladin.asc
with armor), and on decrypt from the stored or derived name \(em but a manual
edit of the field is never overwritten by a later prefill.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
.TP
.B Tab ", " Shift-Tab
Move focus between fields.
.TP
.B \(<- ", " \(->
Switch the mode tab (when the tabs are focused), or change the focused
cipher/KDF selector.
.TP
.B Space
Toggle the focused checkbox or expander.
.TP
.B Enter
Run the selected operation.
.TP
.B Esc
Cancel a running operation, or quit when idle.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
Quit. The terminal is restored first; if an operation is running it is canceled
and the process exits 130.
.TP
.B ?
Toggle the help overlay.
.SH ADVANCED OPTIONS
A collapsible advanced pane (toggled with
.BR Space )
exposes the remaining options. Selecting keyfile-only opens this pane
automatically.
.TP
.B Show password
Unmask the password and confirm fields.
.TP
.B Keyfile-only
The
.B \-\-no\-password
equivalent: requires a keyfile, clears any typed password, and opens the
advanced pane.
.TP
.B Cipher
Encrypt-only selector:
.B aes\-256\-gcm
(default) or
.BR chacha20\-poly1305 .
Changed with
.B \(<-
/
.BR \(-> .
.TP
.B KDF
Encrypt-only selector:
.B argon2id
(default),
.BR scrypt ,
or
.BR pbkdf2 .
The selected KDF's cost knobs are shown beneath it, prefilled and range-checked.
.TP
.B Store filename in header
The
.B \-\-name
switch: store the input's basename in the header (off by default; sensitive).
Encrypt only.
.TP
.B ASCII armor
The
.B \-\-armor
switch: write ASCII-armored (base64) output. Re-derives the output extension.
Encrypt only.
.TP
.B Remove input after success
The
.B \-\-remove
equivalent: best-effort delete the input after a successful operation. A plain
delete with no secure erase. Encrypt and decrypt.
.TP
.B Overwrite existing output
The
.B \-f
equivalent: overwrite an existing output file. By default an existing output is
refused. Encrypt and decrypt.
.TP
.B Keyfile
Path to keyfile material combined with the password. Available in encrypt,
decrypt, and verify. Must be a regular file of 1 byte to 1 MiB.
.SH DIFFERENCES FROM paladin(1)
The TUI shares the CLI's core but differs in input handling, because the
terminal interface owns stdin and stdout:
.TP
.B Paths only
Every path field accepts a filesystem path only. A literal
.B \-
(stdin/stdout streaming) is rejected; the CLI remains the only front-end with
streaming.
.TP
.B No password-file or password-env
There is no
.B \-\-password\-file
or
.BR \-\-password\-env .
The password is typed into the masked field, captured inside the TUI's own event
loop under raw mode (not via a separate prompt).
.SH EXIT STATUS
The TUI uses the same exit-status contract as
.BR paladin (1):
the last completed operation's result maps to the exit status on a normal quit.
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
General or I/O error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or argument error.
.TP
.B 3
Authentication failure (wrong password/keyfile or a corrupted/tampered file).
.TP
.B 4
Unsupported, unknown, or malformed format or header.
.TP
.B 130
Canceled \(em reserved for
.B Ctrl-C
terminating the process while an operation is running.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR paladin (1).
The project specification in
.B docs/DESIGN.md
(section 7 for the terminal interface).
